ound the  world  wherever the british arrived they  developed the railway network and such  was the case for sudan  khartoum became the hub and a transport  point to the red sea and port sudan  two other spurs went respectively north  to darfur and south to cordovan and  elobed  all heavy material must be hoisted on a  flatbed  this electrolux refrigerator is operated  with kerosene and will be indispensable  for storage of field collected specimens  the microbiology department involved in  tropical disease research has utilized  the railroad system for transporting a  laboratory car to various parts of sedan  personal effects and more fragile  equipment are loaded in this car which  will be pooled at the end of the railway  the tarpoolin will provide shelter when  specimens are being brought from the  bush  this magnificent steam locomotive will  reach lobed at the top speed of 35 miles  an hour and there will be only a few  places to fill up the water tank  the engineers are putting the last  nurturing touches of oil  recommendations are made  everything is checked to ensure a safe  voyage  the 250 mile train journey ends at ello  bead in central sudan  this is a road and caravan junction on  the migration paths from west africa  this is also the end of the railroad and  from there the 2 000 mile inland trek to  the nuba mountains will be on a dirt and  sand road too hard or too soft for  driving comfort  short grass savannah thorny bush's  akashas are the main features of the  cordovan province  as the train stops to take on water the  steam is converted to hot water for tea  this truck taking an audacious turn is  heavily loaded with equipment and  personal baggage from the laboratory car  it also carries the field assistance  kasha is the first nuba village to be  reached its round huts gripping the  flanks of a volcanic outcrop are typical  such hillocks cover approximately 3 000  square miles and range from 300 to 3600  feet in height  women are returning to 